Step 1: Create a Project Folder
Create a folder named:
flask-docker-app
Open the folder in Visual Studio Code.
You can also use:

mkdir flask-docker-app
cd flask-docker-app
code .
Step 2: Create app.py
Create a file named:
app.py
Add the following code:
from flask import Flask
app = Flask(__name__)
@app.route('/')
def home():
return '<h1>Hello from Flask Docker Container!</h1>'
@app.route('/about')
def about():
return '<h2>This Flask application is running inside Docker.</h2>'
if __name__ == '__main__':
app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)

Step 3: Create requirements.txt
Create a file named:
requirements.txt
Add:
Flask
Step 4: Create a Dockerfile
Create a file named: Dockerfile
Add the following code:
FROM python:3.12-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt
COPY app.py .
EXPOSE 5000
CMD ["python", "app.py"]

Step 5: Create .dockerignore
Create a file named:
.dockerignore
Add:
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.git/
.gitignore

This prevents unnecessary files from being copied into the Docker image.
Step 6: Check Docker Installation
Open the terminal and execute:
docker –version
If Docker is installed correctly, its version will be displayed.
Also check whether Docker is running:
docker info
Step 7: Build the Docker Image
Make sure you are inside the project folder.
Run:
docker build -t flask-docker-app .
Here:
• docker build → Creates a Docker image.
• -t flask-docker-app → Gives the image a name.
• . → Uses the current folder as the build context.
After successful execution, the Docker image will be created.
Step 8: Check the Docker Image
Run:
docker images
You should see an image similar to:
REPOSITORY TAG IMAGE ID CREATED SIZE
-docker-app latest xxxxxxxxxxxx A few seconds ago
Step 9: Run the Docker Container
Run:
docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name flask-container flask-docker-app
Here:
• -d → Runs the container in the background.
• -p 5000:5000 → Maps the host port to the container port.
• --name flask-container → Gives the container a name.
• flask-docker-app → Specifies the Docker image.
Step 10: Check the Running Container
Run:
docker ps
You should see:
CONTAINER ID IMAGE PORTS
xxxxxxxxxxxx flask-docker-app 0.0.0.0:5000->5000/tcp
This confirms that the Flask application is running inside the Docker container.
Step 11: Open the Application in a Browser
Open a web browser and enter:
http://localhost:5000
Expected output:
Hello from Flask Docker Container!
Step 12: Test the About Page
Open:
http://localhost:5000/about
Expected output:
This Flask application is running inside Docker.
Step 13: View Container Logs
To view the Flask application's output, run:
docker logs flask-container
The logs will show information about the Flask server and incoming requests.
Step 14: Stop the Container
After testing the application, stop the container:
docker stop flask-container
Step 15: Remove the Container
To remove the stopped container:
docker rm flask-container
